//? Title: Newell normals: cyclic vector area
//? Subtitle: Actual edge contributions, winding and a corrected nonplanarity experiment
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Compute a polygon boundary’s oriented vector area and derive a unit direction
when that vector is nonzero. Actual translated cyclic-edge records explain the
method, followed by the original square, triangle and tilted quad. We correct
the old claim that the tilted quad is nonplanar, add a genuinely warped control
and distinguish vector area, plane residuals and physical surface area.
)

\toc

> What the cyclic sum measures

For cyclic vertices p_i, Newell’s original components sum
(y_i−y_next)(z_i+z_next), (z_i−z_next)(x_i+x_next) and (x_i−x_next)(y_i+y_next).
Expanding each component makes its same-vertex products telescope, leaving
n=Σp_i×p_next. Translating all vertices by a common origin does not change this
exact sum, because the added terms telescope too. The canonical implementation
now translates by the first vertex and accumulates the actual cross products in
long double, reducing cancellation from a large common translation.

For a simple planar boundary, n is twice its signed area times a unit
normal; ||n||/2 is its area. Winding reversal changes the sign. For a nonplanar
boundary, the components still measure signed projected areas, but ||n||/2 is a
vector-area magnitude, not the area of an unspecified curved surface or
arbitrary triangulation. A self-intersecting boundary can cancel to zero even
when its lobes have nonzero area. No simplicity or planarity certificate is
inferred from one nonzero vector.

> A tilted triangle and its actual contributing edges

The original tilted triangle has vertices(0,0,0),(1,0,1),(0,1,1). With the first
vertex as origin, only its middle edge contributes a nonzero cross product,
namely(−1,−1,1). Dividing by its norm gives the unit direction(−1,−1,1)/√3. The
diagram reads every actual edge contribution and the actual final unit vector;
it does not reconstruct a second Newell loop.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/newell_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/newell_normal.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto f = dm::algorithms::newell_demo_polygons();
    auto t = dm::algorithms::newell_trace(f[1].polygon);
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    std::print("| actual edge | next vertex | cross x | cross y | cross z |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | " "---: |\n");
    for (auto &e : t.edges) {
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", e.index, e.next,
                   double(e.cross[0]), double(e.cross[1]), double(e.cross[2]));
        d.node(std::to_string(e.index), {0., 3. - 3. * e.index},
               std::format("Actual edge {} cross\n({}, {}, {})", e.index,
                           double(e.cross[0]), double(e.cross[1]),
                           double(e.cross[2])));
    }
    d.node("unit", {8, 0},
           std::format("Actual unit direction\n({:.4f}, {:.4f}, {:.4f})",
                       t.analysis.unit[0], t.analysis.unit[1],
                       t.analysis.unit[2]));
    for (auto &e : t.edges)
        d.edge(std::to_string(e.index), "unit");
    d.save_svg("build/newell_tiny.svg");
}
```
